        <div class="czz-answer-block"><h3>Cozzia vs. Osaki</h3><p>Osaki prioritizes feature density and assertive massage intensity across a broad range. Cozzia prioritizes biometric intelligence and physiological adaptation — doing less, but more precisely. If raw intensity and feature volume matter most, Osaki. If intelligent therapeutic response is your goal, Cozzia — particularly the Quantum or Qi XE Pro.</p></div>
        <div class="czz-answer-block"><h3>Cozzia vs. Kyota</h3><p>Kyota's Syner-D dual-roller technology delivers a distinctive, refined massage feel. Cozzia differentiates through biometric AI — the ability to read your body's stress markers and adapt. Both deliver a more moderate intensity profile than Osaki. The Quantum's Chair Doctor&trade; has no direct equivalent in Kyota's lineup.</p></div>
        <div class="czz-answer-block"><h3>Cozzia vs. Infinity</h3><p>Infinity offers competitive pricing and solid 4D performance with a comfortable, accessible feel. Cozzia's Quantum and Qi XE Pro add a biometric intelligence layer that Infinity's lineup does not replicate. For pure value at accessible prices, Infinity is strong. For AI-driven therapeutic precision, Cozzia is ahead.</p></div>
        <div class="czz-answer-block"><h3>Cozzia vs. Bodyfriend</h3><p>Bodyfriend competes at the luxury design and craftsmanship tier. Their XD Pro roller module delivers a sophisticated, precision-engineered feel. Cozzia's edge is biometric adaptation — Bodyfriend's BIA system is limited to the Pharaoh Q. If interior aesthetics and crafted materials drive the decision, Bodyfriend; if physiological AI adaptation is the priority, Cozzia Quantum.</p></div>
